.TH OROC-UPDATE-SERVER 1 "" "Oro Runtime 0.1.2" "Oro Runtime Manual"
.SH NAME
oroc\-update\-server \- Run an update server that speaks the Oro Application Update Protocol.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
oroc update server [options]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Run an update server that speaks the Oro Application Update Protocol.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-root=<dir>\fR
directory containing manifest trees and artifacts to serve
.TP
\fB\-\-host=<host>\fR
interface to bind (default: 0.0.0.0)
.TP
\fB\-\-port=<port>\fR
TCP/UDP port to bind (default: 8080)
.TP
\fB\-\-manifest\-name=<name>\fR
manifest filename to look up under each appId (default: manifest.json or ORO_UPDATE_MANIFEST_FILENAME)
.TP
\fB\-\-tcp\fR
run in TCP mode (binary OUP CHECK/RESPONSE)
.TP
\fB\-\-udp\fR
run in UDP mode (binary OUP CHECK/RESPONSE)
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-file=<path>\fR
mirror logs to a JSON file
.SH NOTES
.PP
Default mode is HTTP. The server exposes these endpoints.
.IP \(bu 2
GET /health           — readiness metadata
.IP \(bu 2
POST /check           — accepts a CHECK JSON payload with "appId" and responds with a RESPONSE JSON
.PP
whose manifestUrl points at "/<appId>/<manifest\-name>" when present
.IP \(bu 2
GET /<path>           — serves files rooted under \-\-root, including "<appId>/<manifest\-name>"
.PP
and "<appId>/<manifest\-name>.sig" HTTP mode is designed to be run behind a load balancer or reverse proxy in production. TCP and UDP modes implement the same CHECK/RESPONSE selection semantics using the binary OUP framing.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
\fBoroc update server \-\-root ./updates\fR
serve manifests and bundles over HTTP on port 8080
.TP
\fBoroc update server \-\-root ./updates \-\-tcp \-\-port 9090\fR
run a TCP OUP server on port 9090
.TP
\fBoroc update server \-\-root ./updates \-\-udp \-\-port 9090\fR
run a UDP OUP server on port 9090
.SH SEE ALSO
oroc(1), oroc\-update(1)
